export const DE_C1_S04_ENGLISH: Record<string, string[]> = {
  "de-die-kritikerin": [
    `Renate Brandl was considered the most feared restaurant critic in Munich, and she did nothing to soften that reputation. A single sentence from her pen could ruin a restaurant. About a sauce in a Michelin-starred restaurant in Schwabing she had once written that it had "the bearing of a civil servant just before retirement"; three months later the restaurant was up for sale. Cooks recognised her at the cloakroom and went pale. She enjoyed it, without ever admitting it.`,
    `In March she came down with a fever, nothing special, a week in bed. When she got up again, the coffee smelled of nothing. The orange she bit into was wet and cold, nothing else. The ear, nose and throat specialist spoke of a loss of smell after a viral infection; it happened, he said, and her sense of taste might come back, in weeks or months, or it might not. Renate told no one. A critic without taste was like a conductor without hearing, no, worse: a deaf conductor could at least still read the score.`,
    `So she went on writing. She wrote about the light in the dining rooms, the weight of the cutlery, the clatter from the kitchen and the way a waiter set down a plate as if he were handing over a confession. She described the crackle of a crust, the heat of a soup, the colour of a red cabbage that looked like a sunset over Lake Starnberg. What the dishes tasted like, she paraphrased with elegant platitudes. Readers were delighted. Her editor-in-chief praised her for writing more poetically than ever. Renate smiled and hated every single day.`,
    `In June she visited the "Glut", a small restaurant in Giesing with twelve tables and a cook who was only twenty-nine. Tarek Haddad had trained under a well-known chef in Salzburg and cooked what he called "a country inn with wanderlust". Renate ate her way through the entire menu, three days later praised the crackling of the pork crust and the "seductive, almost childlike sweetness" of the crème for dessert, and gave the restaurant four out of five stars. In Giesing the phone didn't stop ringing.`,
    `Tarek read the review three times, and each time he grew more uneasy. That evening there had been an accident in the kitchen: his apprentice had mixed up the containers and made the crème with salt instead of sugar. Tarek only noticed when the plate had already gone out, and had watched, trembling, as the dreaded Frau Brandl raised her spoon. She had eaten the whole bowl without batting an eyelid. At the time he had taken it for politeness. Now he understood.`,
    `He could have turned it into a story that would have amused half of Munich. Instead he wrote her a letter, by hand. He knew, he wrote, and he would tell no one. But he invited her for the following Monday, when the restaurant was closed. "I'll cook you a menu that you don't need to taste." Renate read the letter standing in her hallway and then sat down on the stairs. For two days she wrestled with herself. Then she accepted.`,
    `The evening began with ice-cold, strongly sparkling water that tingled on the tongue like a joke you feel before you understand it. Then came a radish so crunchy that it echoed in the empty room, a soup that was hot and cold at the same time, and finally a piece of lamb with a paste of fresh chillies. Tarek had read that spiciness is perceived not through taste but through pain. Renate took a bite, and for the first time in four months something burned in her mouth. She put down her fork and cried, without being ashamed.`,
    `Her next column appeared a week later. In it she told, without beating about the bush, of her illness and of how she had deceived her readers for months. She announced that she would be taking a break. The last paragraph was about the "Glut" in Giesing and ended with the sentence: "I tasted nothing in this restaurant. It was the best meal of my life."`,
  ],
  "de-die-kartenlegerin-vom-naschmarkt": [
    `Frau Edeltraud Pospischil had worked for thirty-five years at Statistics Austria before she rented a small table with a velvet cloth between an olive stall and a cheese seller at Vienna's Naschmarkt. Card reading, twenty minutes, twenty-five euros. She didn't believe in the cards in the slightest. She believed in probabilities. Most people, she had found, came with an answer they had long known and were only looking for someone to give them permission. Her only rule was: never say anything that wouldn't be true anyway.`,
    `On an icy Tuesday in January a young man sat down at her table, red with cold, with a scarf wound three times round his neck. His name was Florian, he said, he was a teacher at a secondary school in Favoriten, and he had just one question: whether he should propose to his girlfriend. The ring had been lying in his sock drawer for six weeks. "She's just so independent," he said. "And her mother's supposed to be a dragon. I don't even know her. Lena says she takes every man apart like a statistic."`,
    `Frau Pospischil shuffled the cards with calm hands. The real trick was to ask questions without it sounding like questions. That way she learned within a few minutes that the girlfriend was a doctor at the General Hospital, climbed mountains, considered roses an invention of the florists' lobby and, as a matter of principle, never talked to her mother about men. Then Florian took out his phone to show her a photo. Frau Pospischil glanced at it and for a moment lost her composure. Well, I'll be. Smiling at her from the screen was her own daughter. Her Lena, who had apparently kept a boyfriend from her for a year and a half.`,
    `She laid the first card on the table without looking. It was the Tower. Florian went pale. "Don't worry," she said, and her voice sounded almost normal. "It only means something's going to change. That's always the case anyway." Inwardly she went through the situation like a table of figures. She could tell him the truth, and he would throw the ring into the Danube in shock. She could advise him against it, and Lena would never find out and would still never forgive her. Or she could do what she charged twenty-five euros for: tell him what was true anyway.`,
    `"The cards are clear," she said, tapping the Tower as if something were written on it. "No roses. Under no circumstances roses. No restaurant, no violinist. Up a mountain, early in the morning, before the sun rises. The Rax would be ideal." She drew a second card. "And when you go to see the mother: bring Punschkrapfen. She likes them." Florian stared at her. "How do you know about the Punschkrapfen?" Frau Pospischil raised her eyebrows. "The cards, Herr Florian. The cards."`,
    `He wanted to give her thirty euros, but she gave him five back. "Twenty-five," she said. "There's no family discount." Florian laughed uncertainly because he didn't get the joke, and disappeared into the crowd between the stalls. Frau Pospischil watched him go for a long time. Then, an hour early, she packed up her cards and went to the nearest pastry shop, where she ate two Punschkrapfen one after the other, something she otherwise only allowed herself at Christmas.`,
    `Three weeks later, on a Sunday, the bell rang at her flat in Ottakring. Outside stood Lena, beaming, and next to her a young man with a scarf wound three times round his neck and a box of Punschkrapfen in his hands. When he looked her in the face, he went as white as the icing. "Mum, this is Florian," said Lena. "We've got engaged. On Friday, on the Rax, at sunrise. Just imagine, he knew I hate roses." "Well, well," said Frau Pospischil, taking the box from him. "Some men just have a feel for these things. Come in, Florian."`,
    `He has never told Lena about it. But once a year, around the wedding anniversary, Frau Pospischil finds an envelope without a sender in her letterbox. Inside are twenty-five euros and a slip of paper with a single word: "Consultation".`,
  ],
  "de-das-alibi-von-bayreuth": [
    `Detective Chief Inspector Dilek Aydın considered opera a form of assault with advance warning: people dying while singing for hours, and an audience that paid for it into the bargain. All the more annoying, then, that her current case hinged on Richard Wagner of all people. On Thursday evening a jeweller's shop in Nuremberg's old town had been cleaned out between nine and twenty to ten, and the burglar had switched off the alarm with the correct code. Four people knew the code. One of them was Gregor Wendt, the shop's sales manager until the spring, then dismissed without notice.`,
    `Wendt sat opposite her in the interview room, freshly shaved, in a light linen jacket, looking as relaxed as someone who expects a conversation about the weather. He had spent the whole evening in the Bayreuth Festspielhaus, he said, at "Götterdämmerung", from four in the afternoon until almost half past ten. He put his ticket on the table: stalls, row nineteen, scanned at the entrance at 3:41 p.m. "I applied for this ticket for ten years," he said. "Do you seriously think I'd leave before the end?"`,
    `Then he told her about it. He described the set, the costumes, the conductor, the fanfares that are blown from the balcony of the Festspielhaus before each act, the heat in the auditorium, which has no air conditioning, a gentleman in row twelve who had to cough at the very quietest moment. He spoke with a warmth that couldn't be faked, or, thought Dilek, that could be faked very well. Either the man was innocent or he was the best liar who had ever sat across from her. And liars who were so thoroughly prepared usually had a reason for it.`,
    `Her colleague, Inspector Leitner, had been an involuntary Wagnerian since his wedding and confirmed every detail. The cast was right, the production too, and Bayreuth was, after all, a good hour from Nuremberg. "He was there," said Leitner. "Nobody talks like that if he's got it from the programme." But Dilek did what she always did when she liked a story too much: she checked the boring things. She phoned the festival's press office and asked whether anything special had happened that evening.`,
    `The woman on the phone burst out laughing. Whether anything special had happened? At five past nine, in the middle of the third act, a thunderstorm had caused a power cut. For thirty-eight minutes the audience had sat in the dark, nearly two thousand people, and at some point someone in the stalls had started quietly humming the Rhine motif, and in the end half the house had hummed along. It had been, the woman said, the strangest and most beautiful evening she could remember. Since then people in Bayreuth had talked about nothing else.`,
    `At the second interview Dilek asked Herr Wendt to describe the third act to her again, in detail, since she was only a layperson. He did so with pleasure: Siegfried's death, the funeral march, which, he said, had moved him to tears, and the final scene, without a single interruption. "And the thunderstorm?" she asked. He hesitated for no more than the blink of an eye. "Outside? You don't hear anything of that inside." "At 9:05 p.m. the lights went out in the Festspielhaus," said Dilek. "For thirty-eight minutes. Almost two thousand people sat in the dark and hummed. Only you didn't notice."`,
    `The interview room went very quiet. Then Wendt gave up, almost relieved. He had left the Festspielhaus during the second interval; his car had been waiting in a side street. He had seen the production the year before and memorised every detail, just in case. He had armed himself against everything except the weather. Leitner shook his head as Wendt was led away. "Walking out of Götterdämmerung for a few necklaces," he muttered. "That's the real crime."`,
    `That same evening Dilek Aydın sat at her laptop at home and, without really knowing why, filled in an application for tickets to the Bayreuth Festival. She wanted to understand what could make a man wait ten years for a ticket, and two thousand strangers hum together in the dark. The waiting time is said to be several years. She has time. She is a civil servant.`,
  ],
  "de-das-wandbild": [
    `On the morning of 3 October the whole of Kirchheimbach stood in front of the old school in silence, which in a Palatinate wine village happens about as rarely as a solar eclipse. Overnight someone had painted the entire gable wall of the empty building, twelve metres wide, in bold colours: a schoolyard full of children, with satchels, knee socks and plaits, under a mighty chestnut tree that had not existed for decades. Underneath, in narrow letters, was a year: 1961.`,
    `Mayor Rüdiger Kessler arrived last, wearing an anorak over his pyjamas, and looked as if someone had personally spat on his car. The old school, you see, was to be sold to an investor from Mannheim who wanted to turn it into "wine lofts with flair". The village council was due to vote on Friday. "Criminal damage," said Kessler, and then, a little louder, when nobody answered: "It'll be painted over by Friday. End of discussion."`,
    `Nele Hartmann, who had come back from Berlin two years earlier to take over her parents' winery and was still regarded in the village as something of an incomer, stayed longer than the others. She had once been a journalist, and puzzles gave her no peace. The longer she looked, the stranger she found the picture. Whoever had painted it handled a spray can like a professional. But the details came from someone who must have been there: the teacher's dog, a black spitz that had answered to the name of Bello; the chestnut tree that had fallen in a storm in 1979; the old pump in the corner of the yard. Young hands, thought Nele, and an old memory.`,
    `She asked around, at the baker's, in the seasonal wine tavern, at the grape harvest, where people talked about nothing else between the rows anyway. The old people recognised themselves in the picture, one after another, and argued over their wine spritzers in dimpled glasses about who the fat boy with the ball was. And Herr Schäfer, the school's neighbour, remembered a small red car that had been parked behind the gym on the night before the holiday, with a parking permit from the art academy in Mainz on the windscreen. Only one person in Kirchheimbach studied at the art academy in Mainz: Lea Kessler, the mayor's daughter.`,
    `Nele didn't drive to Mainz. She drove to the care home in Bad Dürkheim where the mayor's mother had lived for a year. Hildegard Kessler, eighty-nine, had taught at the village school for forty years. She was sitting by the window, hands in her lap, and under her fingernails, Nele saw it at once, tiny traces of paint were stuck. "I only said what went where," said the old lady before Nele could even ask a question. "The girl did the painting. I sat in the car with a thermos and made sure she got the spitz right."`,
    `On Friday evening the council chamber was fuller than it had been since the debate about the wind turbine. Kessler had the motion to sell in front of him, along with a quote for cleaning the façade. Before he opened the meeting, Nele asked him to step outside with her for a moment and showed him, in the beam of her torch, a spot at the bottom left of the picture that nobody had shown him before. There, smaller than the others, stood a boy with a much too big satchel, crying. A young teacher was holding his hand. Rüdiger Kessler stared at the picture for a long time. It had been his first day at school. He had forgotten it. His mother, evidently, had not.`,
    `The motion to sell was withdrawn that evening, "for further consideration", as the minutes put it. Today the old school is a village community centre with a café, a book corner and, on Thursdays, a painting class for senior citizens run by a student from Mainz. When Nele later asked old Frau Kessler why she hadn't simply given her son a piece of her mind, she only smiled. "You've never been able to tell that boy anything," she said. "With him you always had to paint it on the wall."`,
  ],
  "de-der-fuchs-und-die-kamera": [
    `In the Harz, below the Brocken, where the spruces have died in long grey rows and the young forest is finding its way between the dead trunks, there lived a young fox who, until that autumn, had nothing out of the ordinary about him. He hunted mice, he avoided the hiking trails, he slept while people were awake. Until one morning he passed a tree stump and a little red light flashed, very briefly, like a wink.`,
    `He got a fright, backed away and watched the thing from a safe distance: a green box, strapped to the stump with a belt, with a single glass eye. The old badger, who had lived in the same sett for as long as anyone could remember and believed he knew everything, enlightened him. "A wildlife camera," he grumbled. "People use it to count us. Every few weeks someone in a green jacket comes and fetches the pictures." The fox thought about this for a long time. So one was being seen. Not by a hawk that wanted to eat you, but by someone who simply looked at you. It was a completely new feeling, and he liked it enormously.`,
    `From then on he came every day. He learned that the light was most flattering early in the morning and that he looked best from the front left, with his snout slightly raised. He carried mice in front of the lens that he had caught without being hungry, just to show them off. He practised a leap across the stream, which failed twice and on the third attempt earned him a wet coat and, he hoped, a dramatic picture. What he didn't know: the man in the green jacket was called Jens, worked for the national park and put the best shots on the internet. There the fox now had a name, Fridolin, and more admirers than there were mice in the whole of the Harz.`,
    `The autumn turned cold, and Fridolin grew thin. Anyone who spends half the day posing in front of a camera has only the other half left for hunting, and in November that doesn't go far. The badger watched with concern. "Whoever lives for the eye," he said, "starves in front of it." The fox took this for envy. What did a badger who spent his life underground know about being seen?`,
    `Then, on a frosty morning in December, the red light stopped blinking. Fridolin paced up and down in front of the camera, jumped, sat, raised his snout, turned his left side to the lens. Nothing. The battery was flat, but he couldn't know that. He only knew that nobody was looking at him any more, and it felt as if he had suddenly become transparent. He stayed sitting there until it got dark, and the next day he came back.`,
    `On the third day he noticed that he wasn't alone. On the other side of the stream sat a vixen, just as thin, her snout just as raised, staring at a second green box that hung on a birch and didn't blink either. They looked at each other for the first time, and it was a long, somewhat embarrassed look between two creatures who had just realised that for weeks they had been posing in the same place for nobody. Then the vixen got up, shook herself and trotted into the forest. After a moment's hesitation he followed her. They hunted together until morning, and it was the best hunt of his life.`,
    `In March Jens came with new batteries. When he went through the pictures, he found that Fridolin no longer posed. He only saw him from behind, in passing, at the side of a vixen, and in May in a blurred picture with four cubs, not one of which so much as glanced at the camera. They were, Jens felt, the most beautiful shots he had ever taken. He didn't put them on the internet. Some pictures, he thought, belong to those who don't know they are being seen.`,
  ],
};

